<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP to loopback, and to nothing else.

  The platform default for an app targeting API 28 or later is `cleartextTrafficPermitted="false"`
  for every destination, loopback included. That default is right and `base-config` below keeps it.

  The one exception matches a rule this app already states in two other places, and it exists so
  that all three agree rather than so that anything becomes easier:

    - `ServerEndpoint` accepts `https`, or `http` to a loopback address, and refuses everything
      else. Its reasoning is that the sync server speaks plain HTTP behind a TLS-terminating proxy
      and binds 127.0.0.1 by default, so loopback is the one case where no traffic leaves the
      machine and there is nothing for TLS to protect.
    - `PairingViewModel.sendSeal` applies the same rule before it will POST a sealed bundle.

  Without this file the platform would refuse the connection those two have already agreed to
  allow, and the failure would arrive as an unexplained "cannot reach the server" from inside
  OkHttp. Widening it to any other host would be widening the app's own rule, not this file's.

  On a device, loopback is the device itself. It is reachable from a developer machine through
  `adb reverse tcp:8080 tcp:8080`, which forwards the phone's 127.0.0.1 to the host's, so the
  traffic still never leaves the pair of machines the developer is holding.

  (Note for anyone editing this file: an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen. The rest of
  this codebase uses one as an em dash, and doing so here makes the resource unparseable, which
  surfaces as a Gradle failure in `parseDebugLocalResources`, nowhere near the text that caused it.)
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
